{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"daf79387d386f010","name":"card_event_motherland_calls","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/Soviet/Homefront/events/card_event_motherland_calls","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"MOTHERLAND CALLS!","text":"Add a Soviet unit with Destruction in your deck to the support line. Give it Blitz. Destroy it at end of turn.","flavorText":"Soviet posters were among the most powerful tools of mass communication during WWII. As the German invasion advanced in 1941, the Soviet government unleashed a vast visual campaign to mobilize the population. Artists, often working through press agencies like TASS, created bold images that were quickly reproduced and plastered across cities, factories, and barracks. Their messages ranged from calls to defend the homeland to reminders of duty, sacrifice and unity. These posters featured vivid colors, dramatic compositions, and easily recognizable symbols like the red star, hammer and sickle, and heroic figures of soldiers, workers, and peasants. Designed for immediate impact, they aimed to stir emotion and reinforce resolve. Some artists, like Viktor Koretsky and Irakli Toidze, became well known for their stirring and sometimes brutal imagery. Toidze’s famous work The Motherland Calls became iconic, but it was part of a much broader and continuous campaign. Soviet wartime posters used bold pictures and simple slogans to inspire people during the hardest times of the war. They didn’t just spread government messages, they reminded everyone what they were fighting for and helped keep the country united and determined to survive.","faction":"Soviet","type":"order","rarity":"Unique","cost":3,"operationCost":0,"attack":0,"defense":0},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_event_motherland_calls","title":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_motherland_calls_title","SourceString":"MOTHERLAND CALLS!","LocalizedString":"MOTHERLAND CALLS!"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::order","faction":"EFactionEnum::Soviet","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_motherland_calls_text","SourceString":"Add a Soviet unit with Destruction in your deck to the support line. Give it Blitz. Destroy it at end of turn.","LocalizedString":"Add a Soviet unit with Destruction in your deck to the support line. Give it Blitz. Destroy it at end of turn."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_motherland_calls_history","SourceString":"Soviet posters were among the most powerful tools of mass communication during WWII. As the German invasion advanced in 1941, the Soviet government unleashed a vast visual campaign to mobilize the population. Artists, often working through press agencies like TASS, created bold images that were quickly reproduced and plastered across cities, factories, and barracks. Their messages ranged from calls to defend the homeland to reminders of duty, sacrifice and unity. These posters featured vivid colors, dramatic compositions, and easily recognizable symbols like the red star, hammer and sickle, and heroic figures of soldiers, workers, and peasants. Designed for immediate impact, they aimed to stir emotion and reinforce resolve. Some artists, like Viktor Koretsky and Irakli Toidze, became well known for their stirring and sometimes brutal imagery. Toidze’s famous work The Motherland Calls became iconic, but it was part of a much broader and continuous campaign. Soviet wartime posters used bold pictures and simple slogans to inspire people during the hardest times of the war. They didn’t just spread government messages, they reminded everyone what they were fighting for and helped keep the country united and determined to survive.","LocalizedString":"Soviet posters were among the most powerful tools of mass communication during WWII. As the German invasion advanced in 1941, the Soviet government unleashed a vast visual campaign to mobilize the population. Artists, often working through press agencies like TASS, created bold images that were quickly reproduced and plastered across cities, factories, and barracks. Their messages ranged from calls to defend the homeland to reminders of duty, sacrifice and unity. These posters featured vivid colors, dramatic compositions, and easily recognizable symbols like the red star, hammer and sickle, and heroic figures of soldiers, workers, and peasants. Designed for immediate impact, they aimed to stir emotion and reinforce resolve. Some artists, like Viktor Koretsky and Irakli Toidze, became well known for their stirring and sometimes brutal imagery. Toidze’s famous work The Motherland Calls became iconic, but it was part of a much broader and continuous campaign. Soviet wartime posters used bold pictures and simple slogans to inspire people during the hardest times of the war. They didn’t just spread government messages, they reminded everyone what they were fighting for and helped keep the country united and determined to survive."},"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Unique","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::Homefront","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn"],"soundEnterPlay":{"ObjectName":"SoundWave'Mother_Russia'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Assets/Audio/Misc/OrdersFeb17/Mother_Russia.0"},"historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/MOTHERLAND_CALLS.MOTHERLAND_CALLS","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_soviet","Key":"card_event_motherland_calls_photo","SourceString":"A Soviet street scene dominated by large billboards featuring wartime posters and slogans. Date unknown.","LocalizedString":"A Soviet street scene dominated by large billboards featuring wartime posters and slogans. Date unknown."},"isAttackBuff":true,"isDefenseBuff":true},"variables":[{"name":"unitFound","type":"bool","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_event_motherland_calls","owner":"card_event_motherland_calls_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":fals